faq_heading: Tennis FAQ
faqs:
- q: What does ITF pace rating mean in practice?
  a_paragraphs:
  - Pace 1–2 is slow — the ball grips and sits up, favouring baseline rallies. Pace 4–5 is fast, rewarding serve and volley. Most Indian clubs choose medium, pace 3.
  - We set it with the sand loading in the final coats, so tell us how your members play.
- q: My court has cracks — resurface or rebuild?
  a_paragraphs:
  - Hairline cracks can be filled and recoated. Cracks wider than 3 mm, or ones that reopen every year, mean the base is moving; recoating over them buys eighteen months at best.
  - We will send a written assessment after a site visit rather than sell you a coat that will fail.
- q: How many coats does a resurfacing take?
  a_paragraphs:
  - 'Typically five to eight: crack repair, primer, two to four resurfacer coats, two colour coats, then lines. Around seven to ten working days in dry weather.'
- q: Can one court take both tennis and pickleball?
  a_paragraphs:
  - Yes. We mark pickleball lines in a contrasting colour within the tennis court. Up to four pickleball courts fit inside one tennis enclosure.
cta_heading: New Court, or Resurfacing?
